<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!-- New section, written in the style of FOPP. GNU FDL 1.3; see for-loops/ch-loops.ptx. -->
<section xml:id="sec-changing-arrays">
  <title>Changing Every Element of an Array</title>
  <introduction>

    <p>
      You already know how to change one element of an array with bracket notation:
    </p>

    <program language="javascript">
      <code>
  prices[0] = prices[0] + 1;
      </code>
    </program>

    <p>
      Read the right side first: get the value at index 0, add 1 to it.
      Then the left side: store the result back at index 0.
    </p>

    <p>
      Put that line in a loop, change the <c>0</c> to <c>i</c>, and it changes <em>every</em> element.
    </p>

    <activity xml:id="try-change-1">
      <title>Raising Every Price</title>
      <program xml:id="ac-change-1" interactive="activecode" language="javascript">
      <code>
  let prices = [4, 10, 7, 2];

  for (let i = 0; i &lt; prices.length; i++) {
    prices[i] = prices[i] + 1;
  }

  console.log(prices);
      </code>
    </program>
    </activity>

    <p>
      This kind of loop does not print anything or make anything new.
      It changes the array that is already there.
      After the loop, <c>prices</c> holds the new values.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-change-in-function">
    <title>Inside a Function</title>

    <p>
      Most of the time, a loop like this lives in a function.
      The array and the amount come in as parameters, so the function works for any array and any amount.
    </p>

    <activity xml:id="try-change-2">
      <title>Doubling Inside a Function</title>
      <program xml:id="ac-change-2" interactive="activecode" language="javascript">
      <code>
function doubleAll(numbers) {
  for (let i = 0; i &lt; numbers.length; i++) {
    numbers[i] = numbers[i] * 2;
  }
}

let myNumbers = [1, 5, 10];
doubleAll(myNumbers);
console.log(myNumbers);
      </code>
    </program>
    </activity>

    <p>
      The function does not return anything, but <c>myNumbers</c> still changed.
      That is because the parameter <c>numbers</c> and the variable <c>myNumbers</c>
      point to the same array. When the function changes <c>numbers[i]</c>,
      it is changing the one array they both share.
    </p>

    <p>
      Pay attention to the names in the function.
      Inside the loop, we only use the parameter name <c>numbers</c>.
      We never write <c>myNumbers</c>, and we never write the numbers <c>1</c>, <c>5</c>, or <c>10</c>.
      The function has to work for an array it has never seen.
    </p>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="sec-changing-arrays-check">
    <title>Check Your Understanding</title>

    <exercise xml:id="mc-change-result" label="mc-change-result">
      <statement>
        <p>What does this program print?</p>
        <program language="javascript">
          <code>
  let temps = [70, 65, 80];

  for (let i = 0; i &lt; temps.length; i++) {
    temps[i] = temps[i] - 5;
  }

  console.log(temps);
          </code>
        </program>
      </statement>
      <choices>
        <choice correct="yes">
          <statement><p><c>[65, 60, 75]</c></p></statement>
          <feedback><p>Right. Every element went down by 5.</p></feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement><p><c>[70, 65, 80]</c></p></statement>
          <feedback><p>No. The loop stores a new value back into every index.</p></feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement><p><c>[65, 65, 80]</c></p></statement>
          <feedback><p>No. The loop uses <c>i</c>, not <c>0</c>, so it changes more than the first element.</p></feedback>
        </choice>
      </choices>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="mc-change-which-line" label="mc-change-which-line">
      <statement>
        <p>A function has a parameter <c>list</c> that holds an array of numbers, and a parameter <c>amount</c> that holds a number. Which line, inside a loop over <c>list</c>, adds <c>amount</c> to every element?</p>
      </statement>
      <choices>
        <choice correct="yes">
          <statement><p><c>list[i] = list[i] + amount;</c></p></statement>
          <feedback><p>Right. It reads the element, adds to it, and stores it back in the same place.</p></feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement><p><c>list[i] + amount;</c></p></statement>
          <feedback><p>No. This works out the sum but never stores it anywhere.</p></feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement><p><c>list = list + amount;</c></p></statement>
          <feedback><p>No. You cannot add a number to a whole array this way. Each element has to be changed one at a time.</p></feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement><p><c>i = list[i] + amount;</c></p></statement>
          <feedback><p>No. This changes the loop variable, not the array.</p></feedback>
        </choice>
      </choices>
    </exercise>
  </subsection>
</section>
